implementationPlan.description:
Tests first: port the hunt repro. Create packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/waitForTask.deleteWake.test.ts with the Write tool, EXACTLY as below (the RED gate pins `2 failed | 1 passed (3)` at base 54635828, with (w2) and (w3) failing). The architect dry-ran it there and against the fix. Keep `park()` returning `{ parked }`: an async helper that returned the bare promise would adopt it and block until the wait settled, so (w2) would time out even with the fix.
```ts
import { afterEach, beforeEach, describe, expect, it, vi } from 'vitest';
import { ToolTestHarness } from './toolTestHarness.js';
import type { ToolDefinition } from './index.js';
import { claimNextTaskTool } from './claimNextTask.js';
import { deleteTaskTool } from './deleteTask.js';
import { activeWaiters, waitForTaskTool } from './waitForTask.js';

// task-b7203d89 (hunt #11): a legacy dependent counts a missing dependsOn id as
// satisfied, so delete_task can make it claimable. A parked wait_for_task must
// re-run its match on TASK_DELETED; it re-matched only on TASK_CREATED/UPDATED.
describe('wait_for_task wakes when a delete frees a dependent (task-b7203d89)', () => {
  const h = new ToolTestHarness();

  beforeEach(async () => {
    h.init();
    h.setupMoeFolder();
    h.createEpic();
    h.createTask({ id: 'task-prereq', status: 'PLANNING', order: 1 });
    h.createTask({ id: 'task-dep', status: 'WORKING', dependsOn: ['task-prereq'], order: 2 });
    h.createTask({ id: 'task-other', status: 'PLANNING', order: 3 });
    h.createTask({ id: 'task-gate-a', status: 'PLANNING', order: 4 });
    h.createTask({ id: 'task-gate-b', status: 'PLANNING', order: 5 });
    h.createTask({ id: 'task-dep2', status: 'WORKING', dependsOn: ['task-gate-a', 'task-gate-b'], order: 6 });
    await h.state.load();
  });

  afterEach(() => {
    vi.restoreAllMocks();
    for (const waiter of Array.from(activeWaiters.values())) waiter.unsubscribe();
    activeWaiters.clear();
    h.state.clearEmitter();
    h.cleanup();
  });

  const call = async (tool: ToolDefinition, args: Record<string, unknown>) =>
    (await tool.handler(args, h.state)) as Record<string, unknown>;
  const remove = (taskId: string) => call(deleteTaskTool(h.state), { taskId });
  const tick = () => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 50));
  // Returns the pending wait inside an object: an async function returning the
  // bare promise would adopt it and block until the wait itself settles.
  const park = async () => {
    const parked = call(waitForTaskTool(h.state), { statuses: ['WORKING'], workerId: 'worker-1', timeoutMs: 3000 });
    await tick();
    expect(activeWaiters.has('worker-1')).toBe(true);
    return { parked };
  };
  const stillParked = async () => {
    await tick();
    expect(activeWaiters.has('worker-1')).toBe(true);
  };

  it('(w1) an unrelated delete re-runs the match but keeps the waiter parked', async () => {
    await park();
    await remove('task-other');
    await stillParked();
  });

  it('(w2) deleting the only unmet prerequisite wakes the waiter with the dependent, which the claim then hands out', async () => {
    const { parked } = await park();
    await remove('task-prereq');
    expect(await parked).toMatchObject({ hasNext: true, task: { id: 'task-dep' }, nextAction: { tool: 'moe.claim_next_task' } });
    const claimed = await call(claimNextTaskTool(h.state), { statuses: ['WORKING'], workerId: 'worker-1' });
    expect(claimed).toMatchObject({ hasNext: true, task: { id: 'task-dep' } });
  }, 15_000);

  it('(w3) deleting one of two unmet prerequisites keeps it parked; deleting the second wakes it', async () => {
    const { parked } = await park();
    await remove('task-gate-a');
    await stillParked();
    await remove('task-gate-b');
    expect(await parked).toMatchObject({ hasNext: true, task: { id: 'task-dep2' } });
  }, 15_000);
});
```

The fix in packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/waitForTask.ts, with your Edit tool. Replace only the comment and the event filter in the parked listener, about :439-440:
```ts
          // Only react to task creation/update events
          if (event.type !== 'TASK_CREATED' && event.type !== 'TASK_UPDATED') return;
```
with
```ts
          // Re-match on task creation, update and deletion: a delete can free a
          // dependent, since a legacy consumer counts a missing dependsOn id as met.
          if (event.type !== 'TASK_CREATED' && event.type !== 'TASK_UPDATED' && event.type !== 'TASK_DELETED') return;
```
Leave everything after it alone: the findMatchingTask call, the skippedForNoTeam wake, and the pending-question branch, which keeps its own `event.type === 'TASK_UPDATED'` test so a delete never takes it. Sibling task-c8618ccd (WORKING) edits that pending-question path, so do not reflow it. Its hunks sit about 28 lines below this one, so the landing merge stays clean.
Why not a delete-specific wake: the deleted row may be unrelated ((w1)), or the dependent may still have another unmet prerequisite ((w3)). Only the shared re-match gets both right, and it keeps dependency gating, finalizing holds, profile admission and the team rule in one predicate.
Error handling: no new path. A throw in findMatchingTask is caught and logged by StateManager.emit, exactly as for a create or update; emit drops a subscriber only after 3 throws in a row, and the wait's own timer still resolves it. The delete's payload is never read. taskStore.ts removes the row from state.tasks before it emits TASK_DELETED, so the re-match sees the row gone.
